Key methodologies include analyzing immigrants' wage changes using data from the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) and additional data from the Mexican Migration Project and the Latin American Migration Project. These datasets allow for a comparison of pre- and post-migration wages and examination of selection and skill transfer issues. The research controls for selection effects by focusing on the same workers' earnings in different countries and investigating selection more deeply by comparing immigrants' characteristics to non-migrants.

Results indicate that immigrants from poorer countries experience an average wage gain at migration that is 38% of the GDP per worker gap, suggesting that 62% of the income difference is attributable to human capital. The study also highlights that wage gains are larger for less educated immigrants, supporting the hypothesis of imperfect substitution between skilled and unskilled labor.

